IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hoq-common`

-- Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (Queensland) -- making a health service complaint and how it is first dealt with:
-- part 1 (ss 7, 8, 20-23), part 3 (ss 31, 32, 34, 35, 35A), part 4 (ss 38(2), 39(2), 44), part 5 (ss 47-50),
-- part 6 (ss 53-56) and s 278.
--
-- SEAT: the person who makes a health service complaint (the complainant). Every question is asked as that person
-- would ask it. The health ombudsman's judgments ("is satisfied", "reasonably considers") are inputs, never decided.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Ombudsman Act 2013: making and dealing with a complaint`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 1, Part 3 division 1-2 -- can I complain (ss 7, 8, 20-23, 31, 32)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 7(1) "A health service is a service that is, or purports to be, a service for maintaining, improving, restoring or
-- managing people's health and wellbeing." (3) "A health service includes a support service for a service mentioned in
-- subsection (1)." (4) "Also, without limiting subsection (1), a health service includes-- (a) a service dealing with
-- public health ...; (b) a service providing alternative or complementary medicine; and (c) a service prescribed under
-- a regulation to be a health service." (5) "A health service does not include a service prescribed under a
-- regulation not to be a health service."
GIVEN `it is for health and wellbeing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is a support service for such a service` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is of a kind s 7(4) lists` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a regulation prescribes it not to be a health service` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 7 -- is a health service` `it is for health and wellbeing` `it is a support service for such a service` `it is of a kind s 7(4) lists` `a regulation prescribes it not to be a health service` IF
        (`it is for health and wellbeing` OR `it is a support service for such a service` OR `it is of a kind s 7(4) lists`)
    AND (NOT `a regulation prescribes it not to be a health service`)

-- The provider's standing, ss 8, 20-22. s 8 "A health service provider is-- (a) an individual (a health practitioner)
-- who is-- (i) a health practitioner under the National Law; or (ii) another individual who provides a health
-- service; or (b) an entity, other than an individual, who provides a health service (a health service organisation)."
DECLARE `The provider's standing` IS ONE OF
    `provides health services now`
    `provided health services then, but no longer does (s 20)`
    `was a registered health practitioner then, but is no longer registered (s 21)`
    `is not registered now, but was registered in a health profession under a corresponding prior Act (s 22)`

DECLARE `Can I complain` IS ONE OF
    `yes: any person may make a health service complaint (ss 31, 32)`
    `yes: a complaint may be made on behalf of the person who died (ss 23(1)(a), (2), 32)`
    `yes: about their behaviour while they were a health service provider, as if they still were one (s 20)`
    `yes: about their behaviour while they were registered, as if they still were (s 21)`
    `yes, but only to the extent a complaint about that behaviour could have been made under the corresponding prior Act (s 22(3))`
    `no: s 22 applies only to the extent a complaint could have been made under the corresponding prior Act (s 22(3)(a))`
    `no: the provider does not provide a health service, so this is not a health service complaint (ss 8, 31)`

-- s 31 "A health service complaint is a complaint about a health service or other service provided by a health
-- service provider." s 32 "Any person may make a health service complaint." s 23(1)(a), (2): a person who has died
-- and could have complained: "a health service complaint may be made on behalf of the person". s 23(1)(c), (4): a
-- complaint may be made though the provider has died. ss 20(2), 21(2), 22(2), (3): above.
-- READING (finding 2): under s 31 a complaint may be about "other service" provided by a health service provider, so
-- a service a regulation excludes from s 7 can still be complained of if its provider provides some health service.
GIVEN `the service` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provider provides other health services` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the standing` IS A `The provider's standing`
      `the complaint could have been made under the prior Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person who received the service has died` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I complain`
`ss 20-23, 31, 32 -- can I complain` `the service` `the provider provides other health services` `the standing` `the complaint could have been made under the prior Act` `the person who received the service has died` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`the service` OR `the provider provides other health services`))
    THEN `no: the provider does not provide a health service, so this is not a health service complaint (ss 8, 31)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the standing`
         WHEN `provided health services then, but no longer does (s 20)` THEN `yes: about their behaviour while they were a health service provider, as if they still were one (s 20)`
         WHEN `was a registered health practitioner then, but is no longer registered (s 21)` THEN `yes: about their behaviour while they were registered, as if they still were (s 21)`
         WHEN `is not registered now, but was registered in a health profession under a corresponding prior Act (s 22)` THEN
             IF `the complaint could have been made under the prior Act`
             THEN `yes, but only to the extent a complaint about that behaviour could have been made under the corresponding prior Act (s 22(3))`
             ELSE `no: s 22 applies only to the extent a complaint could have been made under the corresponding prior Act (s 22(3)(a))`
         OTHERWISE
             IF `the person who received the service has died`
             THEN `yes: a complaint may be made on behalf of the person who died (ss 23(1)(a), (2), 32)`
             ELSE `yes: any person may make a health service complaint (ss 31, 32)`

@export Can I make a health service complaint to the Queensland health ombudsman about this service and this provider (Health Ombudsman Act 2013 ss 7, 8, 20-23, 31, 32)?
GIVEN `it is for health and wellbeing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is (or claims to be) the service one for maintaining, improving, restoring or managing people's health and wellbeing (for example treatment by a doctor, nurse, dentist, physiotherapist, hospital or ambulance)?
      `it is a support service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it a support service for such a service (business, clinical, corporate or other support to it)?
      `it is a listed kind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it a public health service (a program to prevent disease or injury or to promote health, such as cancer screening), alternative or complementary medicine, or a service a regulation declares to be a health service?
      `a regulation excludes it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a regulation declared that kind of service not to be a health service?
      `the provider provides other health services` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does (or did) the provider provide some health service, even if the service you complain of is not one?
      `the standing` IS A `The provider's standing` @desc Which describes the provider? (Choose "provides health services now" for a current provider.)
      `the complaint could have been made under the prior Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if the provider was registered under a corresponding prior Act and is not registered now: could a complaint about the same behaviour have been made under that prior Act?
      `the person who received the service has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you complaining about a service given to someone who has since died?
GIVETH A `Can I complain`
`Can I make this health service complaint question` `it is for health and wellbeing` `it is a support service` `it is a listed kind` `a regulation excludes it` `the provider provides other health services` `the standing` `the complaint could have been made under the prior Act` `the person who received the service has died` MEANS
    `ss 20-23, 31, 32 -- can I complain` (`s 7 -- is a health service` `it is for health and wellbeing` `it is a support service` `it is a listed kind` `a regulation excludes it`) `the provider provides other health services` `the standing` `the complaint could have been made under the prior Act` `the person who received the service has died`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 3 division 2 -- will my complaint be accepted, and by when (ss 34, 35, 35A, 278)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 35A "The health ombudsman may decide not to accept a complaint if the health ombudsman is satisfied-- (a) the
-- complaint would be more appropriately dealt with by an entity other than the health ombudsman or an entity to whom
-- the health ombudsman may refer the complaint under this Act; or (b) the complainant has not sought a resolution of
-- the complaint with the relevant health service provider and it is reasonable in the circumstances for the
-- complainant to first seek the resolution." A list without "only": an empty list is "s 35A gives no ground", not "the
-- complaint must be accepted" (fork F5).
DECLARE `Ground for not accepting` IS ONE OF
    `another entity would more appropriately deal with it (s 35A(a))`
    `you have not first sought a resolution with the provider, and it is reasonable for you to do so first (s 35A(b))`

GIVEN `the ombudsman is satisfied another entity would more appropriately deal with it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I have sought a resolution with the provider` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the ombudsman is satisfied it is reasonable for me to seek that first` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `Ground for not accepting`
`s 35A -- grounds for not accepting` `the ombudsman is satisfied another entity would more appropriately deal with it` `I have sought a resolution with the provider` `the ombudsman is satisfied it is reasonable for me to seek that first` MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (IF `the ombudsman is satisfied another entity would more appropriately deal with it` THEN LIST `another entity would more appropriately deal with it (s 35A(a))` ELSE EMPTY),
        (IF (NOT `I have sought a resolution with the provider`) AND `the ombudsman is satisfied it is reasonable for me to seek that first` THEN LIST `you have not first sought a resolution with the provider, and it is reasonable for you to do so first (s 35A(b))` ELSE EMPTY))

@export On which grounds in s 35A may the health ombudsman decide not to accept my complaint? (An empty list means s 35A gives no ground on these facts; it does not say its grounds are the only ones.)
GIVEN `another entity is more appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the health ombudsman satisfied that your complaint would be more appropriately dealt with by some other body (one the ombudsman cannot refer it to)?
      `I tried the provider first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you already tried to resolve the complaint with the provider?
      `it is reasonable to try the provider first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the health ombudsman satisfied that it is reasonable in your circumstances for you to try the provider first?
GIVETH A LIST OF `Ground for not accepting`
`Can my complaint be not accepted question` `another entity is more appropriate` `I tried the provider first` `it is reasonable to try the provider first` MEANS
    `s 35A -- grounds for not accepting` `another entity is more appropriate` `I tried the provider first` `it is reasonable to try the provider first`

-- s 35(1) "Within 7 business days after receiving a complaint, the health ombudsman must-- (a) decide-- (i) to accept
-- the complaint and take particular relevant action ...; or (ii) to accept the complaint and take no further action
-- ...; or (iii) not to accept the complaint; and (b) give notice of the decision to the complainant and relevant health
-- service provider under section 278." (2) "For deciding the number of business days for subsection (1), the following
-- days are not counted-- (a) any business days on which there is an outstanding requirement under section 34; (b) any
-- business days necessary to comply with division 2A for the complaint."
-- READING (fork F3): the days not counted are added to the 7.
GIVEN `the day the complaint was received` IS A DATE
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 35 -- the last day to decide and tell me` `the day the complaint was received` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` MEANS
    `the business day after` (7 PLUS `the business days not counted`) `the day the complaint was received` `the public holidays`

@export By what day must the health ombudsman decide whether to accept my complaint, and tell me (s 35)?
GIVEN `the day the complaint was received` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman receive your complaint?
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc On how many business days were you still to answer a request from the ombudsman for confirmation or more information (s 34), or was the ombudsman consulting Ahpra about a complaint against a registered practitioner (part 3 division 2A)? (0 if none.)
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which weekdays from the day of receipt onward are public holidays in Queensland? (List them, or give none.)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the ombudsman decide whether to accept my complaint question` `the day the complaint was received` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` MEANS
    `s 35 -- the last day to decide and tell me` `the day the complaint was received` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays`

@export Did the health ombudsman decide whether to accept my complaint, and tell me, in time (ss 35, 278)?
GIVEN `the day the complaint was received` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman receive your complaint?
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc On how many business days were you still to answer a request from the ombudsman for confirmation or more information (s 34), or was the ombudsman consulting Ahpra about a complaint against a registered practitioner? (0 if none.)
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which weekdays from the day of receipt onward are public holidays in Queensland? (List them, or give none.)
      `it has been decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the ombudsman decided whether to accept your complaint?
      `the day it was decided` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day was the decision made? (Any date if not.)
      `I have been told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been given notice of the decision?
      `the day I was told` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day were you told? (Any date if not.)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc What is today's date?
GIVETH A `Decision and notice state`
`Was my complaint decided and notified in time question` `the day the complaint was received` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` `it has been decided` `the day it was decided` `I have been told` `the day I was told` `today` MEANS
    `the state of the decision and notice` `the day the complaint was received` `the public holidays` (7 PLUS `the business days not counted`) TRUE `it has been decided` `the day it was decided` `I have been told` `the day I was told` `today`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 4 -- what may be done with my complaint (ss 38(2), 39(2), 44)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 44(1)(b)(iii) "the matter of the complaint arose, and the complainant was aware of the matter, at least 2 years
-- before the complaint was made." (2) "However, subsection (1)(b)(iii) does not apply if the health ombudsman
-- reasonably considers-- (a) a registered health practitioner may have behaved in a way that constitutes professional
-- misconduct; or (b) another ground may exist for the suspension or cancellation of a registered health practitioner's
-- registration." READING (fork F4): "at least 2 years before" is met when the complaint is made on or after the day 24
-- months after the later of the two days.
DECLARE `Is my complaint too old` IS ONE OF
    `the 2-year ground is open: the matter arose, and you knew of it, at least 2 years before you complained; the ombudsman may decide to take no further action on that ground if it reasonably considers so (s 44(1)(b)(iii)). It is not a bar to complaining.`
    `the 2-year ground does not apply: the ombudsman considers there may be professional misconduct or a ground to suspend or cancel registration (s 44(2))`
    `the 2-year ground does not apply: you complained less than 2 years after the matter arose or after you became aware of it (s 44(1)(b)(iii))`

GIVEN `the day the matter arose` IS A DATE
      `the day I became aware of it` IS A DATE
      `the day I complained` IS A DATE
      `the ombudsman considers there may be professional misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the ombudsman considers there may be another ground to suspend or cancel registration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 44(1)(b)(iii), (2) -- the 2-year ground is open` `the day the matter arose` `the day I became aware of it` `the day I complained` `the ombudsman considers there may be professional misconduct` `the ombudsman considers there may be another ground to suspend or cancel registration` IF
        `it arose at least 2 years before`
    AND `I knew at least 2 years before`
    AND (NOT (`the ombudsman considers there may be professional misconduct` OR `the ombudsman considers there may be another ground to suspend or cancel registration`))
    WHERE
        `it arose at least 2 years before` MEANS (24 `months after` `the day the matter arose`) `is on or before` `the day I complained`
        `I knew at least 2 years before` MEANS (24 `months after` `the day I became aware of it`) `is on or before` `the day I complained`

@export Is my complaint old enough that the health ombudsman may decline to deal with it further because the matter arose, and I knew of it, at least 2 years before I complained (s 44(1)(b)(iii), (2))?
GIVEN `the day the matter arose` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the matter you complain about happen?
      `the day I became aware of it` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you become aware of it?
      `the day I complained` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you make (or will you make) the complaint?
      `possible professional misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the health ombudsman reasonably consider that a registered health practitioner may have behaved in a way that is professional misconduct?
      `possible ground to suspend or cancel` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the health ombudsman reasonably consider that some other ground may exist to suspend or cancel a registered practitioner's registration?
GIVETH A `Is my complaint too old`
`Is my complaint too old question` `the day the matter arose` `the day I became aware of it` `the day I complained` `possible professional misconduct` `possible ground to suspend or cancel` MEANS
    IF `possible professional misconduct` OR `possible ground to suspend or cancel`
    THEN `the 2-year ground does not apply: the ombudsman considers there may be professional misconduct or a ground to suspend or cancel registration (s 44(2))`
    ELSE IF `s 44(1)(b)(iii), (2) -- the 2-year ground is open` `the day the matter arose` `the day I became aware of it` `the day I complained` `possible professional misconduct` `possible ground to suspend or cancel`
         THEN `the 2-year ground is open: the matter arose, and you knew of it, at least 2 years before you complained; the ombudsman may decide to take no further action on that ground if it reasonably considers so (s 44(1)(b)(iii)). It is not a bar to complaining.`
         ELSE `the 2-year ground does not apply: you complained less than 2 years after the matter arose or after you became aware of it (s 44(1)(b)(iii))`

-- s 44(1) "At any time, the health ombudsman may decide to take no further action on a health service complaint or
-- other matter if the health ombudsman reasonably considers-- (a) the complaint or other matter-- (i) is frivolous,
-- vexatious, trivial or not made in good faith; or (ii) is misconceived or lacking in substance; or (iii) is being
-- adequately dealt with by another appropriate entity; or (iv) has been resolved or otherwise appropriately finalised
-- ...; or (v) despite reasonable efforts ..., cannot be resolved; or (b) for a complaint-- (i) the complainant has
-- failed, without reasonable excuse, to-- (A) satisfactorily cooperate with attempts made or arranged by the health
-- ombudsman to resolve the complaint; or (B) comply with a request from the health ombudsman for information the
-- health ombudsman needs to properly deal with the complaint; or (ii) the complaint is withdrawn; or (iii) [2 years]."
-- (3) "... if a complainant, health service provider or other relevant person dies and the health ombudsman reasonably
-- considers it would be appropriate to take no further action." Each limb is the ombudsman's judgment: an input.
DECLARE `Ground for no further action` IS ONE OF
    `frivolous, vexatious, trivial or not made in good faith (s 44(1)(a)(i))`
    `misconceived or lacking in substance (s 44(1)(a)(ii))`
    `being adequately dealt with by another appropriate entity (s 44(1)(a)(iii))`
    `already resolved or appropriately finalised (s 44(1)(a)(iv))`
    `cannot be resolved despite reasonable efforts (s 44(1)(a)(v))`
    `you did not cooperate with attempts to resolve it, without reasonable excuse (s 44(1)(b)(i)(A))`
    `you did not comply with a request for information, without reasonable excuse (s 44(1)(b)(i)(B))`
    `you withdrew the complaint (s 44(1)(b)(ii))`
    `the matter arose, and you knew of it, at least 2 years before you complained (s 44(1)(b)(iii))`
    `a complainant, provider or other relevant person has died and it is appropriate to stop (s 44(3))`

GIVEN `g` IS A `Ground for no further action`, `x` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `Ground for no further action`
`the ground` `g` `if` `x` MEANS IF `x` THEN LIST `g` ELSE EMPTY

@export On which grounds in s 44 may the health ombudsman decide, at any time, to take no further action on my complaint? (An empty list means none of the s 44 grounds is open on these facts.)
GIVEN `frivolous or not in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ombudsman reasonably consider the complaint frivolous, vexatious, trivial or not made in good faith?
      `misconceived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ombudsman reasonably consider it misconceived or lacking in substance?
      `dealt with elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ombudsman reasonably consider it is being adequately dealt with by another appropriate body?
      `already resolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ombudsman reasonably consider it has already been resolved or appropriately finalised?
      `cannot be resolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ombudsman reasonably consider it cannot be resolved despite reasonable efforts?
      `I did not cooperate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the ombudsman found that you failed, without reasonable excuse, to cooperate satisfactorily with attempts to resolve it?
      `I did not give information asked for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the ombudsman found that you failed, without reasonable excuse, to comply with a request for information it needs?
      `I withdrew it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you withdrawn the complaint?
      `the day the matter arose` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the matter you complain about happen?
      `the day I became aware of it` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you become aware of it?
      `the day I complained` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you make the complaint?
      `possible professional misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ombudsman reasonably consider that a registered health practitioner may have behaved in a way that is professional misconduct?
      `possible ground to suspend or cancel` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ombudsman reasonably consider that another ground may exist to suspend or cancel a registered practitioner's registration?
      `someone died and stopping is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a complainant, the provider or another relevant person died, and does the ombudsman reasonably consider stopping appropriate?
GIVETH A LIST OF `Ground for no further action`
`On what grounds may the ombudsman take no further action question` `frivolous or not in good faith` `misconceived` `dealt with elsewhere` `already resolved` `cannot be resolved` `I did not cooperate` `I did not give information asked for` `I withdrew it` `the day the matter arose` `the day I became aware of it` `the day I complained` `possible professional misconduct` `possible ground to suspend or cancel` `someone died and stopping is appropriate` MEANS
    concat (LIST
        `the ground` `frivolous, vexatious, trivial or not made in good faith (s 44(1)(a)(i))` `if` `frivolous or not in good faith`,
        `the ground` `misconceived or lacking in substance (s 44(1)(a)(ii))` `if` `misconceived`,
        `the ground` `being adequately dealt with by another appropriate entity (s 44(1)(a)(iii))` `if` `dealt with elsewhere`,
        `the ground` `already resolved or appropriately finalised (s 44(1)(a)(iv))` `if` `already resolved`,
        `the ground` `cannot be resolved despite reasonable efforts (s 44(1)(a)(v))` `if` `cannot be resolved`,
        `the ground` `you did not cooperate with attempts to resolve it, without reasonable excuse (s 44(1)(b)(i)(A))` `if` `I did not cooperate`,
        `the ground` `you did not comply with a request for information, without reasonable excuse (s 44(1)(b)(i)(B))` `if` `I did not give information asked for`,
        `the ground` `you withdrew the complaint (s 44(1)(b)(ii))` `if` `I withdrew it`,
        `the ground` `the matter arose, and you knew of it, at least 2 years before you complained (s 44(1)(b)(iii))` `if` (`s 44(1)(b)(iii), (2) -- the 2-year ground is open` `the day the matter arose` `the day I became aware of it` `the day I complained` `possible professional misconduct` `possible ground to suspend or cancel`),
        `the ground` `a complainant, provider or other relevant person has died and it is appropriate to stop (s 44(3))` `if` `someone died and stopping is appropriate`)

-- s 38(2) "However, the health ombudsman may deal with a complaint by facilitating local resolution of it, or
-- conciliating it, only if-- (a) it relates to the provision of a health service to the complainant; or (b) the
-- complainant has made the complaint on behalf of someone else and the health ombudsman is satisfied the complainant
-- has a sufficient interest in it, for example, because-- (i) ... a child and the complainant is the child's parent;
-- or (ii) ... impaired capacity and the complainant is the person's guardian ...; or (iii) ... a person who has died
-- and the complainant is a member of the person's family; or (iv) ... another person who has asked the complainant to
-- make the complaint." "Only if": outside both limbs the answer is no.
DECLARE `Can it go to local resolution or conciliation` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the complaint is about a health service provided to you (s 38(2)(a))`
    `yes: you complained for someone else and the ombudsman is satisfied you have a sufficient interest (s 38(2)(b))`
    `no: local resolution and conciliation are available only for a service to you, or where the ombudsman is satisfied you have a sufficient interest (s 38(2))`

@export Can my complaint be dealt with by local resolution or conciliation, the two ways that can lead to a settlement with the provider (s 38(2))?
GIVEN `the service was provided to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the health service you complain of provided to you?
      `I complained for someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you make the complaint on behalf of someone else (your child, a person under your guardianship, a family member who died, or someone who asked you to)?
      `the ombudsman is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the health ombudsman satisfied you have a sufficient interest in the complaint?
GIVETH A `Can it go to local resolution or conciliation`
`Can my complaint go to local resolution or conciliation question` `the service was provided to me` `I complained for someone else` `the ombudsman is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` MEANS
    IF `the service was provided to me`
    THEN `yes: the complaint is about a health service provided to you (s 38(2)(a))`
    ELSE IF `I complained for someone else` AND `the ombudsman is satisfied I have a sufficient interest`
         THEN `yes: you complained for someone else and the ombudsman is satisfied you have a sufficient interest (s 38(2)(b))`
         ELSE `no: local resolution and conciliation are available only for a service to you, or where the ombudsman is satisfied you have a sufficient interest (s 38(2))`

-- s 38(1) the relevant actions; s 39(2) "The health ombudsman's power to take relevant action to deal with a health
-- service complaint (other than facilitating local resolution of it or conciliating it) is not affected if the
-- complaint is withdrawn." s 44(1)(b)(ii): withdrawal is a ground for no further action.
DECLARE `Relevant action` IS ONE OF
    `assessment (part 5)`
    `local resolution (part 6)`
    `immediate action against a practitioner (part 7)`
    `investigation (part 8)`
    `a prohibition order (part 8A)`
    `referral to Ahpra or another government body (part 9)`
    `referral to the director of proceedings for QCAT (part 10)`
    `conciliation (part 11)`
    `an inquiry (part 12)`

DECLARE `If I withdraw` IS ONE OF
    `yes: withdrawing does not affect the ombudsman's power to take this action (s 39(2)); the ombudsman may instead decide to take no further action because you withdrew (s 44(1)(b)(ii))`
    `s 39(2) does not keep this power alive after withdrawal (it excepts local resolution and conciliation) and the Act says nothing more; the ombudsman may decide to take no further action because you withdrew (s 44(1)(b)(ii))`

@export If I withdraw my complaint, can the health ombudsman still take this action on it (ss 39(2), 44(1)(b)(ii))?
GIVEN `the action` IS A `Relevant action` @desc Which action are you asking about?
GIVETH A `If I withdraw`
`If I withdraw my complaint can the ombudsman keep dealing with it question` `the action` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the action`
    WHEN `local resolution (part 6)` THEN `s 39(2) does not keep this power alive after withdrawal (it excepts local resolution and conciliation) and the Act says nothing more; the ombudsman may decide to take no further action because you withdrew (s 44(1)(b)(ii))`
    WHEN `conciliation (part 11)` THEN `s 39(2) does not keep this power alive after withdrawal (it excepts local resolution and conciliation) and the Act says nothing more; the ombudsman may decide to take no further action because you withdrew (s 44(1)(b)(ii))`
    OTHERWISE `yes: withdrawing does not affect the ombudsman's power to take this action (s 39(2)); the ombudsman may instead decide to take no further action because you withdrew (s 44(1)(b)(ii))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Parts 5 and 6 -- requests to me, assessment and local resolution (ss 47-50, 53-56)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- ss 47(1)-(2), 53(1)-(2) (submissions) and 48(1)-(2), 54(1)-(2) (information): "within a stated period", which "must
-- be reasonable but must not be more than 10 business days after the notice is given." Whether the period is
-- reasonable is not decided here.
DECLARE `My period to respond` IS ONE OF
    `by the day stated in the notice, which is within the Act's 10 business days` HAS `the stated last day` IS A DATE
    `the notice states a longer period than the Act allows; the Act's 10 business days end on` HAS `the Act's last day` IS A DATE

@export The health ombudsman has sent me a notice inviting submissions or requiring information during an assessment or local resolution: by when must I respond, and does the stated period keep within the Act's limit of 10 business days (ss 47(2), 48(2), 53(2), 54(2))?
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the notice given to you?
      `the last day stated in the notice` IS A DATE @desc What is the last day for your response stated in the notice?
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which weekdays from the notice day onward are public holidays in Queensland? (List them, or give none.)
GIVETH A `My period to respond`
`By when must I give submissions or information question` `the day the notice was given` `the last day stated in the notice` `the public holidays` MEANS
    IF `the last day stated in the notice` `is on or before` `the limit`
    THEN `by the day stated in the notice, which is within the Act's 10 business days` `the last day stated in the notice`
    ELSE `the notice states a longer period than the Act allows; the Act's 10 business days end on` `the limit`
    WHERE
        `the limit` MEANS `the business day after` 10 `the day the notice was given` `the public holidays`

-- ss 47(3), 53(3) "The health ombudsman must consider each submission received within the stated period." ss 48(3),
-- 54(3) "The person must comply with the notice unless the person has a reasonable excuse. Maximum penalty--50 penalty
-- units." (4) "It is a reasonable excuse for an individual not to give information that giving the information might
-- tend to incriminate the individual."
DECLARE `Kind of notice` IS ONE OF
    `an invitation to make submissions (s 47 or 53)`
    `a requirement to give stated information or documents (s 48 or 54)`

DECLARE `Must I respond` IS ONE OF
    `no: submissions are invited, not required; any you give within the stated period must be considered (ss 47(3), 53(3))`
    `no: it is a reasonable excuse that the information might tend to incriminate you (ss 48(4), 54(4))`
    `not if your excuse is a reasonable one (ss 48(3), 54(3)); failing without one may also let the ombudsman take no further action on your complaint (s 44(1)(b)(i)(B))`
    `yes: you must comply; the maximum penalty is 50 penalty units (ss 48(3), 54(3)), and failing may let the ombudsman take no further action on your complaint (s 44(1)(b)(i)(B))`

@export Must I give the health ombudsman what its notice asks for during an assessment or local resolution, and what if I do not (ss 47, 48, 53, 54)?
GIVEN `the kind of notice` IS A `Kind of notice` @desc Does the notice invite submissions, or require you to give stated information or documents?
      `it might incriminate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Might giving the information tend to incriminate you?
      `I have another excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you have some other excuse for not giving it (whether it is reasonable is for a court)?
GIVETH A `Must I respond`
`Must I give the information required question` `the kind of notice` `it might incriminate me` `I have another excuse` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of notice`
    WHEN `an invitation to make submissions (s 47 or 53)` THEN `no: submissions are invited, not required; any you give within the stated period must be considered (ss 47(3), 53(3))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `it might incriminate me`
        THEN `no: it is a reasonable excuse that the information might tend to incriminate you (ss 48(4), 54(4))`
        ELSE IF `I have another excuse`
             THEN `not if your excuse is a reasonable one (ss 48(3), 54(3)); failing without one may also let the ombudsman take no further action on your complaint (s 44(1)(b)(i)(B))`
             ELSE `yes: you must comply; the maximum penalty is 50 penalty units (ss 48(3), 54(3)), and failing may let the ombudsman take no further action on your complaint (s 44(1)(b)(i)(B))`

-- s 49(1) "The health ombudsman must complete the assessment within 22 business days after deciding to carry out the
-- assessment." (2) "However, the health ombudsman may extend the period ... by a further period of up to 22 business
-- days if necessary because of-- (a) the size or complexity of the complaint; or (b) the time taken to obtain
-- submissions ... or information ...". (3) "... any business days on which the health ombudsman is awaiting the outcome
-- of a notification given under section 35J ... are not counted." s 55(1)-(3) is the same for local resolution ("must
-- try to resolve ... within 22 business days after deciding to try local resolution"), with its own grounds.
-- An extension of more than 22 business days is not one the Act allows: only 22 is counted (fork F6).
GIVEN `the day of the decision to start` IS A DATE
      `the business days of extension` IS A NUMBER
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`ss 49, 55 -- the business days allowed` `the day of the decision to start` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` MEANS
    22 PLUS (`the smaller of` `the business days of extension` `and` 22) PLUS `the business days not counted`

GIVEN `the day of the decision to start` IS A DATE
      `the business days of extension` IS A NUMBER
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`ss 49, 55 -- the last day` `the day of the decision to start` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` MEANS
    `the business day after` (`ss 49, 55 -- the business days allowed` `the day of the decision to start` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays`) `the day of the decision to start` `the public holidays`

@export By what day must the health ombudsman complete the assessment of my complaint (s 49)?
GIVEN `the day it decided to assess` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman decide to assess your complaint?
      `the business days of extension` IS A NUMBER @desc By how many business days has the ombudsman extended the assessment because of the size or complexity of the complaint or the time taken to get submissions or information? (0 if none; at most 22 counts.)
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Only for a complaint against a registered practitioner: on how many business days was the ombudsman waiting for Ahpra's answer to a proposal to take no further action (s 35J)? (0 if none.)
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which weekdays in the period are public holidays in Queensland? (List them, or give none.)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the assessment be completed question` `the day it decided to assess` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` MEANS
    `ss 49, 55 -- the last day` `the day it decided to assess` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays`

-- s 50: after completing the assessment, decide and give notice under s 278. READING (fork F7): the assessment is
-- complete when the s 50 decision is made, so the decision must fall within the s 49 period; the notice then has the
-- 5 business days of s 278.
@export Was the assessment of my complaint completed and decided in time, and was I told of the decision in time (ss 49, 50, 278)?
GIVEN `the day it decided to assess` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman decide to assess your complaint?
      `the business days of extension` IS A NUMBER @desc By how many business days has the ombudsman extended the assessment? (0 if none; at most 22 counts.)
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc On how many business days was the ombudsman waiting for Ahpra's answer under s 35J? (0 if none.)
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which weekdays in the period are public holidays in Queensland? (List them, or give none.)
      `it has been decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the ombudsman made its decision after the assessment?
      `the day it was decided` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day? (Any date if not.)
      `I have been told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been given notice of the decision?
      `the day I was told` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day? (Any date if not.)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc What is today's date?
GIVETH A `Decision and notice state`
`Was the assessment decided and notified in time question` `the day it decided to assess` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` `it has been decided` `the day it was decided` `I have been told` `the day I was told` `today` MEANS
    `the state of the decision and notice` `the day it decided to assess` `the public holidays` (`ss 49, 55 -- the business days allowed` `the day it decided to assess` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays`) FALSE `it has been decided` `the day it was decided` `I have been told` `the day I was told` `today`

@export By what day must the health ombudsman try to resolve my complaint by local resolution with the provider (s 55)? If it is not resolved by then, the ombudsman must decide what to do next and tell me (s 56).
GIVEN `the day it decided to try local resolution` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman decide to try local resolution?
      `the business days of extension` IS A NUMBER @desc By how many business days has the ombudsman extended local resolution (because submissions or information took time, or because it believes the complaint may yet be resolved)? (0 if none; at most 22 counts.)
      `the business days not counted` IS A NUMBER @desc On how many business days was the ombudsman waiting for Ahpra's answer under s 35J? (0 if none.)
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which weekdays in the period are public holidays in Queensland? (List them, or give none.)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the ombudsman try to resolve my complaint locally question` `the day it decided to try local resolution` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays` MEANS
    `ss 49, 55 -- the last day` `the day it decided to try local resolution` `the business days of extension` `the business days not counted` `the public holidays`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Part 19 -- notice of a decision on my complaint (s 278)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 278(1) above. (4) "A requirement under this Act to give a notice under this section to a complainant does not
-- apply if the complainant has advised the health ombudsman (orally or in writing) that the complainant waives
-- compliance with the requirement." The decisions it applies to: ss 35, 50, 56, 90, 105, 142, 147, 148.
DECLARE `Was I told in time` IS ONE OF
    `no notice is owed to you: you waived it (s 278(4))`
    `yes: you were told within 5 business days after the decision (s 278(1))`
    `no: you were told, but after the 5 business days had ended (s 278(1))`
    `not yet due: you have not been told, but the 5 business days have not ended`
    `overdue: the 5 business days have ended and you have not been told (s 278(1))`

@export Was I told in time of a decision the health ombudsman made on my complaint (to take some action, take no further action, or not accept it), with its reasons (s 278)?
GIVEN `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the health ombudsman make the decision?
      `I waived notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you tell the ombudsman (orally or in writing) that you did not need to be given notice?
      `I have been told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been given notice of the decision?
      `the day I was told` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day? (Any date if not.)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc What is today's date?
      `the public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Which weekdays after the decision are public holidays in Queensland? (List them, or give none.)
GIVETH A `Was I told in time`
`Was I told of the decision in time question` `the day of the decision` `I waived notice` `I have been told` `the day I was told` `today` `the public holidays` MEANS
    IF `I waived notice`
    THEN `no notice is owed to you: you waived it (s 278(4))`
    ELSE IF `I have been told`
         THEN IF `the notice is in time under` (`tell the complainant of the decision` `the day of the decision` `the public holidays`) (`the number of days from` `the day of the decision` `until` `the day I was told`)
              THEN `yes: you were told within 5 business days after the decision (s 278(1))`
              ELSE `no: you were told, but after the 5 business days had ended (s 278(1))`
         ELSE IF `today` `is on or before` (`the business day after` 5 `the day of the decision` `the public holidays`)
              THEN `not yet due: you have not been told, but the 5 business days have not ended`
              ELSE `overdue: the 5 business days have ended and you have not been told (s 278(1))`
